data.content:
## What Happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, **implicit span conversions** make arrays implicitly convertible to `ReadOnlySpan<T>`. This changes overload resolution: `array.Contains(num)` now resolves to `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of the previous `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

The expression tree gets built referencing the span-based method, but the expression tree **interpreter** (`preferInterpretation: true`) cannot execute operations involving `ReadOnlySpan<T>` (a ref struct), so it throws at runtime.

## How to Fix

Force resolution back to `Enumerable.Contains` by either calling it explicitly or casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr = (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 2: Cast to IEnumerable<int> so the span overload isn't preferred
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr = (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);
```

Either approach ensures the compiler picks the `IEnumerable<T>`-based overload, which the expression tree interpreter can handle.
